course: dictionary
lesson: 4395
title: Hairdresser: a person and a place
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A hairdresser is a person whose job is to cut, wash, colour and style people's hair. The word can also mean the shop where they work, called the hairdresser's: «We are going to the hairdresser's after school.»
explain: Use the words around the word to decide which meaning fits. If the sentence is about a job, it means the person. If it is about going somewhere, it means the shop. Check by swapping in the word shop: if it still makes sense, you have the place meaning.
explain: The example «Self-employed people work for themselves, like many plumbers and hairdressers; they choose their hours but get no sick pay or holiday pay and must work out and pay their own tax» shows that a self-employed hairdresser runs their own work. They choose their hours, but they must pay their own tax.
fact: A hairdresser is a person whose job is to cut, wash, colour and style people's hair.
fact: The hairdresser's is the shop where a hairdresser works.
fact: A barber is a hairdresser who cuts mainly men's and boys' hair.
fact: You can book an appointment, which means choosing a time to have your hair done.
fact: Hairdressers are an example of a service job, because they help people.
exercise: What does a hairdresser do? | Fixes cars and bicycles | Delivers letters to front doors | *Cuts, washes, colours and styles people's hair
exercise: In «Self-employed people work for themselves, like many plumbers and hairdressers; they choose their hours but get no sick pay or holiday pay», what do self-employed hairdressers NOT get? | Their own hours | The freedom to work for themselves | *Sick pay or holiday pay
exercise: A hairdresser who cuts mainly men's and boys' hair is often called a what? | Butcher | Banker | Baker | *Barber
test: Where would you go to have your hair cut and styled? | The library | *The hairdresser's | The baker's
test: What can you do to make sure a hairdresser has time for you? | Read a comic | Pay a fine | *Book an appointment
exam: In which sentence does hairdresser mean the shop, not the person? | She works as a hairdresser on the high street. | *We are going to the hairdresser's after school. | My hairdresser cut my hair very short.
